h2. Description

{{OakServlet}} authenticates incoming HTTP requests by decoding the {{Authorization: Basic <base64>}} header and parsing the decoded {{user-id:password}} string. The current implementation is:
{code:java}
String authorization = request.getHeader("Authorization");
if (authorization != null && authorization.startsWith("Basic ")) {
    String[] basic =
            Base64.decode(authorization.substring("Basic ".length())).split(":");
    credentials = new SimpleCredentials(basic[0], basic[1].toCharArray());
} else {
    throw new LoginException();
}
{code}
(File: {{{}oak-http/src/main/java/org/apache/jackrabbit/oak/http/OakServlet.java{}}}, inside {{{}service(...){}}}.)

This violates [RFC 7617|https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7617] ("The 'Basic' HTTP Authentication Scheme"), which states that the credentials are the concatenation of the user-id, a single colon ({{{}:{}}}) character, and the password. The user-id _must not_ contain a colon, but the password _may_ contain any character, including colons. Therefore everything after the *first* colon is the password.

Two distinct defects result from using {{String.split(":")}} with no limit:
h3. 1. Password truncation / weakened authentication (CWE-287)

{{split(":")}} splits on *every* colon and the code keeps only element {{{}[1]{}}}. A password that contains one or more colons is therefore silently truncated to the substring between the first and second colon. The repository then authenticates the user against this shortened secret.

*Example:* a user whose password is {{p4ss:w0rd:!}} is authenticated using only {{p4ss}} (the substring between the first and second colon). The effective secret the server verifies is shorter than the one the user actually set, reducing the keyspace an attacker must search and breaking the user's expectation about password strength.
h3. 2. Unhandled exception -> HTTP 500 (instead of 401)

If the decoded value contains no colon (e.g. a client sends just a user-id, or an empty password {{user:}} - {{String.split}} drops trailing empty strings), accessing {{basic[1]}} throws {{{}ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException{}}}. This runtime exception is *not* caught by the surrounding handlers (which only catch {{LoginException}} and {{{}NoSuchWorkspaceException{}}}), so it escapes as an HTTP 500 with a stack trace rather than a proper 401 Unauthorized challenge.
h2. Steps to reproduce

*Defect 1 (password truncation):*
 # Create a user whose password contains a colon, e.g. {{{}p4ss:w0rd:!{}}}.
 # Send a request with: {{Authorization: Basic <base64 of "user:p4ss:w0rd:!">}}
 # Observe that authentication is evaluated against {{{}p4ss{}}}, not the full password. A login that should fail with the wrong password can succeed, and the full password is never the one actually checked.

*Defect 2 (HTTP 500):*
 # Send a request with an Authorization header whose decoded value has no colon, e.g. Base64 of {{{}nocolon{}}}: {{Authorization: Basic bm9jb2xvbg==}}
 # Observe an HTTP 500 ({{{}ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException{}}}) instead of a clean 401 Unauthorized.

h2. Expected behaviour
 * The password is taken as everything after the first colon, preserving any colons it contains (RFC 7617 compliant).
 * A missing/empty/non-Basic Authorization header, or a decoded value with no colon, results in a 401 Unauthorized challenge - never an HTTP 500.

h2. Actual behaviour
 * Passwords containing colons are truncated at the first colon, weakening authentication.
 * A decoded value without a colon yields HTTP 500 ({{{}ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException{}}}) instead of 401.

h2. Impact
 * *Security:* effective passwords are silently weakened whenever they contain a colon; the secret actually verified is shorter than the one configured.
 * *Robustness/availability:* malformed credential headers produce 500 errors and leak a stack trace instead of returning a standard 401 challenge.

h2. Proposed fix

Split only on the first colon ({{{}indexOf(':'){}}} + {{{}substring{}}}), reject a missing colon with a {{LoginException}} (mapped to 401 by the existing handler), and reject a missing/non-Basic header the same way. The credential parsing could be extracted into a small private helper for clarity, for example:
{code:java}
private Credentials parseBasicCredentials(String authorization)
        throws LoginException {
    if (authorization == null || !authorization.startsWith("Basic ")) {
        throw new LoginException(
            "Missing or unsupported Authorization header; expected Basic scheme");
    }
    String decoded = Base64.decode(authorization.substring("Basic ".length()));
    int colon = decoded.indexOf(':');
    if (colon < 0) {
        throw new LoginException(
            "Malformed Basic credentials: missing ':' separator");
    }
    String userId = decoded.substring(0, colon);
    String password = decoded.substring(colon + 1);
    return new SimpleCredentials(userId, password.toCharArray());
}
{code}
This is a contained bug fix with no API change, so no feature toggle is required.

In [https://github.com/apache/jackrabbit-oak/blob/03499ab99de715f054b3ed0e9787c928d9fb2858/oak-core/src/test/java/org/apache/jackrabbit/oak/security/authentication/token/TokenCleanupTest.java#L100]

 
{code:java}
org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.security.authentication.token.TokenCleanupTest#testBatchSizeLimitsCleanup{code}
 

 
TokenProviderImpl.cleanupExpired runs opportunistically on every createToken call whenever shouldRunCleanup(token) is true (~1/8 random chance).
 
In the test, the cleanup threshold (5) equaled the number of expired tokens created, so the 5th expired-token creation could randomly trigger an extra cleanup batch. When that happened, two batches ran and all 5 expired tokens were removed instead of only batchSize (3), producing the intermittent failure.
 
The fix would be to raise the cleanup threshold to expiredCount + 1 so cleanup can never fire during the expired-token creation phase, guaranteeing exactly one cleanup batch during the extras loop. This makes the batch-size assertion deterministic.

With function-based indexes we can index the upper-case version of a property. But we currently can't index properties conditionally, for example index the path, but only if a certain property exists.

We don't want to add many functions, but but such a feature would be useful. Example:

{noformat}
if(exists([alias]), path(), null)
{noformat}

{{AbstractGarbageCollectionStrategy.cleanup(Context)}} can run without a preceding local compaction (e.g. {{StandbyClientSync}} after sync, {{{}FileStoreBackupImpl{}}}/{{{}FileStoreBackupRestoreMBean{}}}). In that case it builds a placeholder via {{{}CompactionResult.skipped(...){}}}, whose {{requiresGCJournalEntry()}} is hard-coded to {{{}false{}}}:
{code:java}
static CompactionResult skipped(...) {
    return new CompactionResult(currentGeneration, gcCount) {
        // requiresGCJournalEntry() not overridden -> always false
    };
}
{code}
So {{GCJournal.persist(...)}} is never called for this path, even though the reclaimer *is* actively removing segments from older generations. On a cold standby, {{currentGeneration}} legitimately advances on every synced generation, but {{gc.log}} stays frozen and keeps pointing at a stale, already-superseded root.
h3. Impact
 * Standby's own {{gc.log}} never gains new entries after boot, even though sync + cleanup keep reclaiming segments from newer generations.
 * After promoting the standby to primary, later compaction/cleanup cycles trust this stale {{gc.log}} entry, and reads of already-reclaimed segments throw {{{}SegmentNotFoundException{}}}.
 * Same exposure via the always-registered {{FileStoreBackupRestoreMBean}} on *any* live instance.

h3. Steps to reproduce
 # Set up primary + cold standby.
 # Run several rounds of content + online cleanup on primary so multiple generations/gc.log entries exist.
 # Let standby sync + auto-cleanup after each round.
 # Standby's {{segmentstore/gc.log}} shows no new entries despite "cleanup completed... space reclaimed" logs and on-disk generation clearly advancing.
 # Promote standby to primary, keep running cleanup — eventually {{SegmentNotFoundException}} on segments already reclaimed while {{gc.log}} was frozen.

h3. Suggested fix
{code:java}
@Override
boolean requiresGCJournalEntry() {
    return currentGeneration.isCompacted();
}
{code}
 * {{GCJournal.persist}} already no-ops when the generation is unchanged, so this can't cause duplicate/bloated entries.
 * Fresh, never-compacted repos are unaffected ({{{}isCompacted(){}}} is false at generation 0).
 * {{{}succeeded(){}}}/{{{}aborted(){}}} paths untouched.
 * Verified experimentally: previously 100%-reproducible SNFE after standby promotion dropped to 0 occurrences across repeated cycles with this change, and standby {{gc.log}} correctly advanced after every sync+cleanup round.

h2. Summary

{{MapEntry.compareTo()}} in oak-segment-tar builds a brand-new {{Comparator}} chain (3 allocated objects: {{comparingLong}} + {{thenComparing}} + a {{Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder())}} wrapper) on {*}every single pairwise comparison{*}, instead of building it once and reusing it.

{code:java}
// oak-segment-tar/src/main/java/org/apache/jackrabbit/oak/segment/MapEntry.java:150-155
@Override
public int compareTo(@NotNull MapEntry that) {
    return Comparator.comparingLong((MapEntry me) -> me.getHash() & HASH_MASK)
            .thenComparing(MapEntry::getName)
            .thenComparing(MapEntry::getValue, Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()))
            .compare(this, that);
}
{code}

This is the same anti-pattern as OAK-12378 ({{PropertyTemplate.compareTo()}}), fixed by hoisting the comparator chain into a {{static final Comparator<MapEntry>}} field. This method is invoked via {{Arrays.sort}}/{{ComparableTimSort}} when persisting a {{MapRecord}} (see {{RecordWriters$MapLeafWriter.writeRecordContent}} and {{DefaultSegmentWriter$SegmentWriteOperation.writeMapLeaf}}), i.e. whenever a node's children are written as a persisted map — relevant to any node with enough children to use {{MapRecord}} rather than a flat list.

h2. Evidence

Found while profiling a bulk parallel-copy migration workload (JFR + async-profiler, ~793K nodes, 4 worker threads, Java assertions disabled to avoid unrelated noise). {{MapEntry.compareTo}} accounted for 14 of 31,263 total sampled leaf-frame weight in the async-profiler CPU capture, and 12 of 8,930 JFR execution samples with it as the top-of-stack frame — a smaller contributor than {{PropertyTemplate.compareTo}} in OAK-12378 (that recording was during compaction, a much more write-record-sorting-heavy workload), but the identical allocation pattern, confirmed via the following representative stack:

{code:java}
java.util.Arrays.sort(Object[])
java.util.ComparableTimSort.sort(...)
java.util.ComparableTimSort.binarySort(...)
org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.segment.MapEntry.compareTo(MapEntry)
org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.segment.MapEntry.compareTo(Object)
java.util.Comparator.thenComparing(...)
java.util.Comparator.comparing(...)
org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.segment.RecordWriters$MapLeafWriter.writeRecordContent(...)
org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.segment.DefaultSegmentWriter$SegmentWriteOperation.writeMapLeaf(...)
org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.segment.DefaultSegmentWriter$SegmentWriteOperation.writeMap(...)
{code}

Confirmed present verbatim (lines 150-155) on current {{apache/jackrabbit-oak}} trunk (commit {{44851487c5e4d0f9b9b46a40da79d9c78a3c83b9}}), not just an internal fork.

h2. Suggested fix

Same shape as the OAK-12378 fix: hoist the {{Comparator}} chain into a {{private static final Comparator<MapEntry>}} field built once, and have {{compareTo}} delegate to it. No behavior change; existing {{MapEntry}} tests should cover {{compareTo}} unchanged.

