A query with a "name()=''" restriction ignores this restriction.
It is not allowed to have such names, but instead of dropping the restriction (which will allow too many results), the restriction should be used to mean "no entries", specially if there is a function-based index on "name()".

Example:
{noformat}
SELECT * FROM [nt:base] WHERE NAME() = '' 
{noformat}

When an Oak Lucene index definition configures a function-based index (e.g. function = "first([sling:alias])") without also defining a separate property index on the underlying property (e.g. sling:alias), updates to that property on existing nodes are not detected during incremental or synchronous indexing.

Root Cause:
In IndexDefinition.java (lines 1395-1406):
When a property definition has pd.function != null, it is added to functionRestrictions and "continue;" is called. It is never added to propDefns (which populates propConfigs).
As a result:
1. indexingRule.isIndexed(propertyName) returns false for the property used by the function.
2. When the property is modified on an existing node, FulltextIndexEditor.propertyChanged() checks indexingRule.isIndexed(name). Since it returns false, propertiesChanged remains false.
3. In FulltextIndexEditor.leave(), the node is skipped and not re-indexed.

Resolution / Workaround:
Adding a dedicated property definition for the underlying property (e.g. name = "sling:alias", propertyIndex = true) alongside the function definition ensures indexingRule.isIndexed("sling:alias") returns true, allowing FulltextIndexEditor to detect changes on existing nodes and reindex the function value synchronously on commit.

Automated Tests:
Two unit tests demonstrating both the failure and resolution have been added to HybridIndexTest.java:
1. testSlingResourceResolverFunctionIndexAloneDoesNotDetectPropertyUpdate:
Shows that when only the function is configured, updating sling:alias on existing nodes is not detected or re-indexed.
2. testSlingResourceResolverSyncIndex:
Shows that declaring sling:alias as a property rule alongside the function index enables synchronous re-indexing on commit across multiple update phases.

 
{code:java}
@Test
public void testSlingResourceResolverFunctionIndexAloneDoesNotDetectPropertyUpdate() throws Exception {
    Tree idx = root.getTree("/").getChild("oak:index").addChild("slingResourceResolver-funcOnly");
    idx.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, "oak:QueryIndexDefinition", Type.NAME);
    idx.setProperty("type", "lucene", Type.STRING);
    idx.setProperty("compatVersion", 2L, Type.LONG);
    idx.setProperty("async", List.of("async", "sync"), Type.STRINGS);
    idx.setProperty("evaluatePathRestrictions", true, Type.BOOLEAN);
    idx.setProperty("includedPaths", List.of("/content"), Type.STRINGS);
    idx.setProperty("queryPaths", List.of("/content"), Type.STRINGS);

    Tree indexRules = idx.addChild("indexRules");
    indexRules.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, JcrConstants.NT_UNSTRUCTURED, Type.NAME);

    Tree ntBase = indexRules.addChild("nt:base");
    ntBase.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, JcrConstants.NT_UNSTRUCTURED, Type.NAME);

    Tree properties = ntBase.addChild("properties");
    properties.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, JcrConstants.NT_UNSTRUCTURED, Type.NAME);

    // ONLY function property definition, NO explicit sling:alias property definition
    Tree aliasProp = properties.addChild("alias");
    aliasProp.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, JcrConstants.NT_UNSTRUCTURED, Type.NAME);
    aliasProp.setProperty("name", "sling:alias", Type.STRING);
    aliasProp.setProperty("function", "first([sling:alias])", Type.STRING);
    aliasProp.setProperty("propertyIndex", true, Type.BOOLEAN);
    aliasProp.setProperty("ordered", true, Type.BOOLEAN);

    root.commit();

    runAsyncIndex();

    setTraversalEnabled(false);

    // 1. Initial creation of content: indexed because node is new (!before.exists())
    Tree node1 = createPath("/content/node1");
    node1.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("alias1", "extra1"), Type.STRINGS);

    Tree node2 = createPath("/content/node2");
    node2.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("alias2", "extra2"), Type.STRINGS);

    root.commit();

    String query = "select [jcr:path] from [nt:base] where isdescendantnode('/content') and first([sling:alias]) >= '' order by first([sling:alias])";
    assertThat(explain(query), containsString("/oak:index/slingResourceResolver-funcOnly"));

    // Initial creation is queryable
    assertQuery(query, List.of("/content/node1", "/content/node2"));

    // 2. Update sling:alias on existing nodes
    node1.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("aliasZ", "extra1"), Type.STRINGS);
    node2.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("aliasA", "extra2"), Type.STRINGS);
    root.commit();

    // Because sling:alias is not in propDefns, isIndexed("sling:alias") was false.
    // The update was NOT re-indexed on commit: the query still returns the old order [node1, node2]
    // rather than the updated order [node2 (aliasA), node1 (aliasZ)].
    List<String> result = executeQuery(query, "JCR-SQL2");
    assertEquals("Without sling:alias property definition, existing node property update is not detected",
            List.of("/content/node1", "/content/node2"), result);
}

@Test
public void testSlingResourceResolverSyncIndexWithPropertyIndexFalse() throws Exception {
    runSlingResourceResolverSyncTest("slingResourceResolver-propIdxFalse", propAlias -> {
        propAlias.setProperty("propertyIndex", false, Type.BOOLEAN);
    });
}

@Test
public void testSlingResourceResolverSyncIndexWithIndexFalse() throws Exception {
    runSlingResourceResolverSyncTest("slingResourceResolver-indexFalse", propAlias -> {
        propAlias.setProperty("index", false, Type.BOOLEAN);
    });
}

@Test
public void testSlingResourceResolverSyncIndexWithPropertyIndexTrue() throws Exception {
    runSlingResourceResolverSyncTest("slingResourceResolver-propIdxTrue", propAlias -> {
        propAlias.setProperty("propertyIndex", true, Type.BOOLEAN);
    });
}

private void runSlingResourceResolverSyncTest(String indexName, java.util.function.Consumer<Tree> propAliasConfigurator) throws Exception {
    Tree idx = root.getTree("/").getChild("oak:index").addChild(indexName);
    idx.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, "oak:QueryIndexDefinition", Type.NAME);
    idx.setProperty("type", "lucene", Type.STRING);
    idx.setProperty("compatVersion", 2L, Type.LONG);
    idx.setProperty("async", List.of("async", "sync"), Type.STRINGS);
    idx.setProperty("evaluatePathRestrictions", true, Type.BOOLEAN);
    idx.setProperty("includedPaths", List.of("/content"), Type.STRINGS);
    idx.setProperty("queryPaths", List.of("/content"), Type.STRINGS);

    Tree indexRules = idx.addChild("indexRules");
    indexRules.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, JcrConstants.NT_UNSTRUCTURED, Type.NAME);

    Tree ntBase = indexRules.addChild("nt:base");
    ntBase.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, JcrConstants.NT_UNSTRUCTURED, Type.NAME);

    Tree properties = ntBase.addChild("properties");
    properties.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, JcrConstants.NT_UNSTRUCTURED, Type.NAME);

    // Property definition for sling:alias so changes to sling:alias mark the node as changed
    Tree propAlias = properties.addChild("propAlias");
    propAlias.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, JcrConstants.NT_UNSTRUCTURED, Type.NAME);
    propAlias.setProperty("name", "sling:alias", Type.STRING);
    propAliasConfigurator.accept(propAlias);

    Tree aliasProp = properties.addChild("alias");
    aliasProp.setProperty(JcrConstants.JCR_PRIMARYTYPE, JcrConstants.NT_UNSTRUCTURED, Type.NAME);
    aliasProp.setProperty("function", "first([sling:alias])", Type.STRING);
    aliasProp.setProperty("propertyIndex", true, Type.BOOLEAN);
    aliasProp.setProperty("ordered", true, Type.BOOLEAN);

    root.commit();

    // Run initial index update to initialize the index tracker on empty repo (no reindex)
    runAsyncIndex();

    setTraversalEnabled(false);

    // 1. Initial content: alias as an array
    Tree node1 = createPath("/content/node1");
    node1.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("alias1", "extra1"), Type.STRINGS);

    Tree node2 = createPath("/content/node2");
    node2.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("alias2", "extra2"), Type.STRINGS);

    Tree node3 = createPath("/content/node3");
    node3.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("alias3"), Type.STRINGS);

    // Commit content
    root.commit();

    String query = "select [jcr:path] from [nt:base] where isdescendantnode('/content') and first([sling:alias]) >= '' order by first([sling:alias])";
    assertThat(explain(query), containsString("/oak:index/" + indexName));

    // Query immediately without running async indexing
    assertQuery(query, List.of("/content/node1", "/content/node2", "/content/node3"));

    // 2. Only update alias in test (using array)
    node1.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("alias5", "extra1"), Type.STRINGS);
    node2.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("alias0", "extra2"), Type.STRINGS);
    node3.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("alias4"), Type.STRINGS);

    // Commit updates
    root.commit();

    // Query immediately after commit without any async indexing run
    // New order by first([sling:alias]): node2 (alias0), node3 (alias4), node1 (alias5)
    assertQuery(query, List.of("/content/node2", "/content/node3", "/content/node1"));

    // 3. Another update phase: only update sling:alias array properties again
    // (demonstrating new array values and array element reordering)
    node1.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("aliasA", "aliasZ"), Type.STRINGS);
    node2.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("aliasZ", "alias0"), Type.STRINGS);
    node3.setProperty("sling:alias", List.of("aliasB", "aliasC"), Type.STRINGS);

    // Commit updates
    root.commit();

    // Query immediately after commit without any async indexing run
    // New order by first([sling:alias]): node1 (aliasA), node3 (aliasB), node2 (aliasZ)
    assertQuery(query, List.of("/content/node1", "/content/node3", "/content/node2"));

    // Also verify seek-based query works synchronously against the newly updated data
    String seekQuery = "select [jcr:path] from [nt:base] where isdescendantnode('/content') and first([sling:alias]) >= 'aliasB' order by first([sling:alias])";
    assertQuery(seekQuery, List.of("/content/node3", "/content/node2"));
}
{code}

*Context.* 
Our application embeds Oak 2.8.0 (DocumentNodeStore on MongoDB, outside OSGi) and runs on Kubernetes. Since nothing schedules revision GC outside OSGi, it had never run; we introduced a daily maintenance job that runs blob GC (MarkSweepGarbageCollector) and then revision GC (VersionGarbageCollector.gc(24h)), guarded by a cluster-wide lock so that it runs on one instance at a time. Every new pod gets a new cluster id, so over months of deployments the root document accumulated 153 entries in _sweepRev (only 1 active cluster node).

*Symptom.* 
On the first runs blob GC completed in a couple of minutes, but revision GC never did: runs were still going after 1h51m and 12h43m, one core at 100% and no Mongo activity, until a deployment restarted the process. Meanwhile the lock stayed held and other maintenance tasks failed.

The "versionGC" settings document was never created, i.e. no iteration ever completed, although the repository is small (~64k documents, 1,063 split documents). The official oak-run 2.8.0 "revisions collect" on a copy of the database behaves the same: --timeLimit has no effect and SIGTERM does not stop it.

Thread dumps always show 260+ nested LazyIteratorChain frames:
{code:java}
org.apache.commons.collections4.iterators.LazyIteratorChain.updateCurrentIterator(LazyIteratorChain.java:149)
org.apache.commons.collections4.iterators.LazyIteratorChain.hasNext(LazyIteratorChain.java:84)
org.apache.commons.collections4.iterators.LazyIteratorChain.hasNext(LazyIteratorChain.java:86)
... (262-267 nested LazyIteratorChain frames)
org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.plugins.document.SplitDocumentCleanUp.disconnect(SplitDocumentCleanUp.java:62)
org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.plugins.document.VersionGCSupport.deleteSplitDocuments(VersionGCSupport.java:165)
org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.plugins.document.VersionGarbageCollector$GCJob.collectSplitDocuments(VersionGarbageCollector.java:1093)
{code}
*Cause.* 
Since OAK-11451 (Guava removal, 1.78.0; 1.76.0 is not affected), MongoVersionGCSupport.identifyGarbage() chains the results of its queries in a loop:
{code:java}
Iterable<NodeDocument> allResults = emptyList();
for (Bson query : queries) {
    ...
    allResults = IterableUtils.chainedIterable(allResults, iterable);
}
{code}
When DEFAULT_NO_BRANCH is collected, createQueries() returns one query per sweep revision entry plus one final query, so the chain is nested once per cluster id. Guava's Iterables.concat flattened nested concatenations; commons-collections4 LazyIteratorChain.hasNext() calls hasNext() twice on the nested iterator, so an element returned by the first queries costs ~2^(N+1) hasNext() calls (known issue COLLECTIONS-722). In this repository the DEFAULT_NO_BRANCH split documents belong to low cluster ids, i.e. to the most deeply nested queries.

*Fix.* 
Collect the per-query iterables in a list and chain them once:
{code:java}
List<Iterable<NodeDocument>> results = new ArrayList<>(queries.size());
for (Bson query : queries) {
    ...
    results.add(iterable);
}
return IterableUtils.chainedIterable(results);
{code}
*Verification.*
 * Patched oak-run 2.8.0, same command and data: GC completes in 4.1 s (local copy) / 4.0 min (original database, --delay 0.5) with splitDocGCCount=986, intermediateSplitDocGCCount=55; repository content verified unchanged.
 * Synthetic reproduction (MongoDB, N cluster ids each with a DEFAULT_NO_BRANCH split document):
||N||stock 2.8.0||patched||
|20|0.58 s|< 0.5 s|
|26|12.6 s|< 0.5 s|
|28|50.3 s|< 0.5 s|
|30|timeout (180 s)|< 0.5 s|
|153|does not complete|1.4 s|

 * Regression test: MongoRevisionGCTest#identifyGarbageWithManySweepRevisions (40 sweep revisions, 1 DEFAULT_NO_BRANCH document) times out after 30 s without the fix and passes with it.

No other occurrence of the "x = chainedIterable(x, ...)" pattern exists in the code base.

When an archive of a segment store has no binary references entry ({{.brf}}), or the entry can't be read, {{TarReader.getBinaryReferences()}} and {{SegmentArchiveReader.getBinaryReferences()}} return {{null}} (see OAK-12527). The operations that use the binary references of an archive then lose them:
 * *Data store GC*: {{TarReader.collectBlobReferences}} returns without reporting any reference for that archive. The mark phase misses the binaries referenced only from it, and the sweep can delete binaries that are still in use.
 * *Compaction cleanup*: when {{TarReader.sweep}} rewrites such an archive, it copies no binary references, and {{TarWriter}} writes an empty {{.brf}} into the new archive. The references are lost permanently, and later data store GC runs read the empty entry without any warning.
 * *Segment store migration*: {{SegmentStoreMigrator}} (Azure) and {{AwsSegmentStoreMigrator}} skip the missing binary references silently, and the archive is migrated without a {{.brf}} entry. When the target is a TAR store, reading the binary references of that archive failed with the following error, which looks like data corruption although the segments are intact:
{noformat}
java.io.IOException: org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.segment.file.tar.binaries.InvalidBinaryReferencesIndexException: Unrecognized magic number
    at org.apache.jackrabbit.oak.segment.file.tar.SegmentTarReader.getBinaryReferences(SegmentTarReader.java:147)
{noformat}
Since OAK-12527 the missing entry is reported as {{null}}, so the migrated archive is affected by the data store GC and cleanup problems above.

An archive can have no usable {{.brf}} when:
 * a remote archive (Azure, AWS) is still open, or the Oak process terminated before closing it, because its binary references are written only when it is closed;
 * a TAR archive was written without it, e.g. by {{SegmentStoreMigrator}} from such a remote archive;
 * the entry is corrupt.

TAR recovery doesn't cover this case, because it only runs for archives without a valid index.

*Fix*: recover the binary references from the segments of the archive with the store's {{TarRecovery}} when the {{.brf}} entry is missing or can't be read:
 * data store GC collects the recovered references;
 * compaction cleanup writes the recovered references of the remaining segments into the rewritten archive;
 * the migrators recover them with a read-only store of the source, so the source is never modified, and always write a {{.brf}} into the target archive.
